::: {.doc-subject}

Extract a subgraph containing the specified edges and their endpoint nodes.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L54001-L54108){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.edge_subgraph(edges)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Edge-induced subgraph](../assets/graphs/edge-subgraph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="The same six-node graph with its edges numbered 1 to 7; edges 1, 4 and 5 are requested. The result keeps exactly those three accent edges and their endpoints A, B, D, E. Edge 3 between A and D is dropped even though both endpoints stay, which is what separates this from an induced subgraph." width="100%"}

Returns a new graph containing only the specified edges and the nodes that are endpoints of
those edges. This is useful when you want to focus on specific relationships rather than
specific nodes.

Unlike `subgraph()`, which keeps every edge among the chosen nodes, this keeps only the
edges you list. Two kept nodes can therefore lose an edge between them if that edge was
not requested, and nodes that touch none of the listed edges are left out entirely. Common
reasons to use it:

- isolating one type of relationship, such as only inhibitory interactions in a
  signaling network
- keeping the edges that pass a weight or confidence threshold, along with just the nodes
  they connect
- extracting a path, tree, or matching found by another algorithm as its own graph

The original graph is not modified. Nodes and edges keep their IDs, and the selection
state is not carried over.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[edges]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   List of edge IDs to include in the subgraph, as found in the `id` column of
    `get_edge_df()` or returned by `get_edge_ids()`. Order does not matter, and repeated
    IDs are included once. An empty list gives an empty graph.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   New graph containing only the specified edges and their endpoint nodes. Preserves
    directedness, weightedness, and node and edge IDs, labels, weights and attributes.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If any edge ID is not found in the graph.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~subgraph`) 

:   Extract the induced subgraph on a set of nodes.

[](`~transform_to_subgraph_ws`) 

:   Extract the subgraph defined by the current selection.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

The edge-induced subgraph keeps the given edges and their endpoints. Unlike `subgraph()`, it
drops the other edges between those endpoints. Here the two `"inhibits"` edges are kept,
and the `"activates"` edge between `A` and `B` is gone, though both nodes stay:

::: {#412fc6ce .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

g = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_nodes(["A", "B", "C", "D"])
g.add_edges([
    ("A", "B", {"rel": "activates"}),
    ("B", "C", {"rel": "inhibits"}),
    ("C", "D", {"rel": "activates"}),
    ("A", "D", {"rel": "inhibits"}),
])

edf = g.get_edge_df()
inhibits = edf.filter(rel="inhibits")["id"].to_list()

g.edge_subgraph(inhibits).get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['B->C', 'A->D']
```
:::
:::


### The bridges between two clubs

`ec.friends(snapshot=2)` is a network of friendships among the members of a chess club and
a climbing club. Six friendships cross between the clubs, among 8 people. The edge
subgraph keeps just those 6 ties:

::: {#ddc4dbea .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
friends = ec.friends(snapshot=2)
club = friends.get_node_attrs("club")
cross = [
    e for e, s, t in friends.get_edges_as_tuples() if club[s] != club[t]
]

bridges = friends.edge_subgraph(cross)
bridges.count_nodes(), bridges.count_edges()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(8, 6)
```
:::
:::


The subgraph on the same 8 people would also keep the 4 friendships among them within a
club:

::: {#4d0d7927 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
friends.subgraph(bridges.get_node_ids()).count_edges()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
10
```
:::
:::


::: {#5557c07e .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
bridges.plot(color_by="club", label_by="label", seed=1)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}
